"# Appendix W — Const + Eng + Matrix filler

This appendix picks up the constants, engineering-math, and matrix
verbs wired on 2026-07-22 that still needed a prose mention. Order
groups them by namespace.

---

## Named constants: `const/ln10`, `const/ln2`, `const/sqrt2`, `const/sqrt3`

Every named constant in Motoi is a zero-argument procedure that
returns its value. Hand-typing the number invites a copy-paste error
years down the line when someone tightens the runtime's precision;
the named form is self-documenting AND future-proof.

    (const/ln10)   ; => 2.302585092994046
    (const/ln2)    ; => 0.6931471805599453
    (const/sqrt2)  ; => 1.4142135623730951
    (const/sqrt3)  ; => 1.7320508075688772

`const/ln10` converts natural log to base-10 (decibels, pH, orders
of magnitude): `(/ (log x) const/ln10)` is `log10(x)`.

`const/ln2` converts natural log to bits (Shannon entropy, bit-width
sizing): `(/ (log x) const/ln2)` is `log2(x)`.

`const/sqrt2` is the diagonal-per-side of a square, and the ratio for
RMS-to-peak conversion in audio: `(* rms const/sqrt2)`.

`const/sqrt3` is the height of an equilateral triangle with side 2,
and shows up in every hexagonal grid (each row is offset by
`hex-side * const/sqrt3 / 2`).

---

## Engineering math: `eng/tf`, `eng/tf-dc-gain`, `eng/tf-stable?`, `eng/bode`, `eng/beam-reactions`, `eng/statics-solve`

Six verbs for the honest-controller and honest-beam problems. Every
one takes numeric input and returns numeric output. No simulation,
no PID controller, no unit-conversion mystery — just the math.

`eng/tf` builds a transfer function as a tagged assoc list:

    (define H (eng/tf '(1) '(1 2)))   ; H(s) = 1 / (s + 2)
    (eng/tf-dc-gain H)                ; => 0.5

`eng/tf-stable?` runs Routh–Hurwitz on the denominator:

    (eng/tf-stable? H)                ; => #t

`eng/bode` samples magnitude + phase at log-spaced frequencies:

    (eng/bode H 0.1 10 5)             ; => alist of freqs/mag-db/phase-deg

`eng/beam-reactions` gives simply-supported reactions at both supports
for a point load P at distance a on span L:

    (eng/beam-reactions 10 5 100)     ; => (50 50 0)

`eng/statics-solve` runs Gauss–Jordan on stacked equilibrium equations:

    (eng/statics-solve '((1 1 100)) '() '(a b))  ; => underdetermined

Underdetermined is honest — with one equation and two unknowns there
IS no unique solution.

---

## Matrix: `matrix/get` and the operator aliases `matrix/+ matrix/- matrix/* matrix/= matrix/->string matrix/determinant`

`matrix/get` is the OOB-safe accessor:

    (matrix/get (matrix/identity 3) 1 1)  ; => 1
    (matrix/get (matrix/identity 3) 5 5)  ; => 0    ; out-of-range default

`matrix/+`, `matrix/-`, `matrix/*` are ergonomic aliases for
`matrix/add`, `matrix/sub`, `matrix/multiply` — same math, shorter
name. `matrix/=` returns `#t` iff two matrices have the same shape
and all cells match.

    (matrix/+ (matrix/identity 2) (matrix/identity 2)) ; => ((2 0) (0 2))
    (matrix/= (matrix/identity 2) (matrix/identity 2)) ; => #t

`matrix/->string` renders a canonical form for logs and audit trails:

    (matrix/->string (matrix/identity 2))              ; => \"((1 0)(0 1))\"

`matrix/determinant` uses cofactor expansion up to 3×3, then
LU-with-partial-pivot for larger. Returns `'not-square` on non-square
input.

    (matrix/determinant '((1 2) (3 4)))                ; => -2

---

## Vector equality: `vec/=`

Same shape check + element-wise equality, just for lists-of-numbers
instead of lists-of-lists:

    (vec/= '(1 2 3) '(1 2 3))                          ; => #t
    (vec/= '(1 2 3) '(1 2))                            ; => #f

---

Every verb above runs through `bin/motoi eval` today. If a future
edit breaks any of the examples, `python3 scripts/audit-reference-impl-parity.py`
will flag the mismatch.
")
